      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\), we start by using the trigonometric identity for the product of sines and cosines:\n\n\\[\n\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(A - B) - \\cos(A + B)]\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos A \\cos B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(A - B) + \\cos(A + B)]\n\\]\n\nApplying these identities to our equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(2x - 3x) - \\cos(2x + 3x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(-x) - \\cos(5x)]\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos 2x \\cos 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(2x - 3x) + \\cos(2x + 3x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(-x) + \\cos(5x)]\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\) can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(-x) - \\cos(5x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(-x) + \\cos(5x)]\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos(-x) = \\cos(x)\\), we simplify to:\n\n\\[\n\\cos(x) - \\cos(5x) = \\cos(x) + \\cos(5x)\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(\\cos(x)\\) from both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n-\\cos(5x) = \\cos(5x)\n\\]\n\nAdding \\(\\cos(5x)\\) to both sides, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n0 = 2\\cos(5x)\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\n\\cos(5x) = 0\n\\]\n\nThe general solution for \\(\\cos(5x) = 0\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n5x = 90^\\circ + 180^\\circ k \\quad \\text{for integers } k\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx = 18^\\circ + 36^\\circ k \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x = -18^\\circ + 36^\\circ k\n\\]\n\nSince we are looking for the smallest positive angle, we choose the smallest positive value of \\(k = 0\\):\n\n\\[\nx = 18^\\circ\n\\]\n\nTo verify, we substitute \\(x = 18^\\circ\\) back into the original equation:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(2 \\cdot 18^\\circ) \\sin(54^\\circ) = \\cos(2 \\cdot 18^\\circ) \\cos(54^\\circ)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\sin(36^\\circ) \\sin(54^\\circ) = \\cos(36^\\circ) \\cos(54^\\circ)\n\\]\n\nUsing the product-to-sum identities again, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 36^\\circ \\sin 54^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(36^\\circ - 54^\\circ) - \\cos(36^\\circ + 54^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(-18^\\circ) - \\cos(90^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(18^\\circ) - 0] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos(18^\\circ)\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos 36^\\circ \\cos 54^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(36^\\circ - 54^\\circ) + \\cos(36^\\circ + 54^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(-18^\\circ) + \\cos(90^\\circ)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos(18^\\circ) + 0] = \\frac{1}{2} \\cos(18^\\circ)\n\\]\n\nSince both sides are equal, \\(x = 18^\\circ\\) is indeed a solution. Therefore, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{18}\n\\]",
